  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "Write requires flush, but this unbuffered fault-injection fixture\n tests write_all, replacement, and cleanup. write_and_replace does\n not request flushing or durability; real temporary files are\n unbuffered too. A flush call would change the tested operation\n sequence, so the unused callback is an explicit guard."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/src/storage.rs` mod[tests]/impl[FaultyTemporary/Write]/fn[flush] is valid. It sits on an explicit function with a body, and its fenced commentary gives a substantive reason.\n\n- **Why it is never executed:** `flush` exists only because the `Write` trait requires it. Its body panics, so it is an intentional guard.\n- **The commentary matches the source:**\n  - `write_and_replace` calls only `write_all`, then `replace`, then `cleanup` on failure. It never calls `flush` and never requests durability.\n  - The fixture records exactly the operations `write`, `replace` and `cleanup`.\n  - The test `writing_and_replacing_report_each_io_failure_and_cleanup_failure` asserts those operation sequences. A `flush` call would change them, so the guard has a purpose and the zero coverage is acceptable.\n- **The reason is not mere difficulty:** the explanation says that flushing is not part of the tested operation and that the guard would fail if the code started flushing.\n\nI found no gap.",
